PyTorch (ROCm/pytorch release/2.8, commit d618db8): size index_put's
launches by the warp size its device code was compiled with.

WHY
index_put_ with accumulate=True (x.index_put_(idx, v, accumulate=True),
x[idx] += v through torch.Tensor.index_put_, and the backward of advanced
indexing) returns wrong sums on gfx803 whenever indices repeat: against a
float64 CPU reference the GPU is off by up to 4.9 with 1000 indices into
1000 slots and by 175 with 3 million (the CPU's error is below 3e-4). To
reproduce: idx = torch.randint(0, 997, (1000,)); v = torch.randn(1000);
compare torch.zeros(1000).index_put_((idx,), v, accumulate=True) on the
GPU and the CPU.

The launcher in Indexing.cu sizes blocks, grids and shared memory with
at::cuda::warp_size(), the hardware's 64 on gfx803, while the kernels
(indexing_backward_kernel, _stride_1, _small_stride and the quantized
one) index in warps of C10_WARP_SIZE, which c10/macros/Macros.h makes 32
in device code for every AMD target except gfx9. The ROCm grid assigns
indices_per_block * 64 sorted indices to each block, but each block's
warps cover blockIdx.x * blockDim.y * 32 + threadIdx.y * 32 + lane, so
half of the duplicate runs are never summed and lanes 32-63 repeat the
work of lanes 0-31. This is the same host/device disagreement as
gfx803-layer-norm-warp-size.patch, and it is fixed the same way.

WHAT
index_put_warp_size() returns 32 on a ROCm device whose wavefront is 64
but which is not gfx9 (gfx6-8, matching Macros.h), and
at::cuda::warp_size() everywhere else. Both launchers (index_put_ with
accumulate, and its quantized variant) use it. The kernels are unchanged
and correct with 32-wide blocks on 64-lane hardware: each half-wavefront
is one of their warps, their shared memory is indexed by threadIdx.y *
C10_WARP_SIZE, and their shuffle-down reductions read only the result in
lane 0, which depends on lanes 0-31 alone. CUDA builds and gfx9 are
unchanged.

REAL-HARDWARE VERIFICATION (RX 590, this image)
index_put_ with accumulate against a float64 CPU reference, 1 to 3 million
repeated indices into 1000 slots: the GPU now agrees with the CPU (largest
difference 2.5e-4 at 3 million, which is the CPU's own float32 error; the
GPU's is 4e-5). tools/correctness-suite/pytorch/rocprim_ops_check.py 118 of
118. PyTorch op suite 118 of 118, SD 1.5 UNet check stages 1 and 2, conv
checks fp32 and fp16 0 bad, 20 LoRA training steps (median 4 s/step, loss
0.219), 0 GPU faults.

